\newlabel{sec:loss}{{3.7}{4}{}{subsection.3.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:loss}{{13}{4}{}{equation.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:ste}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Fixed-$k$ vs.\ Adaptive Gumbel-Sigmoid STE} (Phase 1 Final vs.\^^MPhase 1 Adaptive). \emph {Left:} the standard fixed-budget pipeline: scores + Gumbel noise feed both a soft sigmoid (for backward pass) and a hard top-$k{=}144$ selection (for forward pass), composed via the STE identity $\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {STE}}{=}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {hard}}{-}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {soft}}.\mathrm {detach}(){+}\mathbf {m}_{\mathrm {soft}}$. \emph {Right:} the adaptive variant inserts a per-sample threshold $\theta $ derived from the BudgetPredictor's $k_{\mathrm {frac}}$ output. The shifted soft mask $\sigma ((\mathbf {n}{-}\theta )/\tau )$ produces a differentiable effective count $k_{\mathrm {eff}}{=}\sum _i m_{\mathrm {soft},i}$ that drives the budget loss; per-sample top-$k_{\mathrm {hard}}$ provides the hard mask with right-padding to $k_{\max }$ for batched processing}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:loss}{{2}{5}{Loss function components}{table.2}{}}
\newlabel{sec:training}{{3.8}{5}{}{subsection.3.8}{}}
\newlabel{tab:hparam}{{3}{5}{Training hyperparameters per phase. P1-F = Phase 1 Final; P1-A = Phase 1 Adaptive. The expanded LoRA target set in P1-F (FFN layers) is critical — it allows the LLM to adapt to merged token representations}{table.3}{}}
\newlabel{sec:efficiency}{{4}{5}{}{section.4}{}}
\citation{kaplan2020scaling}
\citation{goyal2017vqav2}
\citation{hudson2019gqa}
\citation{li2023pope}
\newlabel{fig:arch}{{3}{6}{\textbf {TC-ViTok system architecture with adaptive budget.} Three frozen encoders (purple) extract complementary representations: CLIP ViT-L/14 produces high-resolution patch tokens $\vfine $ for the LLM, DINOv2-S provides a coarse spatial map $\vcoarse $ for the TPN, and CLIP Text encodes the question into $\qemb $. The TPN (887K) cross-attends $\qemb $ over $\vcoarse $ and emits per-position scores $\sN $ plus a context vector $\mathrm {ctx}$. In adaptive mode, the BudgetPredictor (25K, orange) maps $\mathrm {ctx}$ to a per-sample token fraction. The Adaptive Gumbel-STE produces variable-length selection indices, which gate-and-merge $\vfine $ before the multimodal projector and LLaVA-1.5-7B+LoRA}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:merge}{{4}{6}{\textbf {Informative Token Merge (parameter-free).} \textbf {(a)} Each unselected token (gray) is soft-assigned to the most similar selected token (blue) via cosine similarity. Edge thickness encodes $\tilde {A}_{ij}{=}w_i\,\cos (v_i,v_j)$, where $w_i$ is the unselected token's TPN score (numbers below each token). \textbf {(b)} Merged outputs (gradient: selected~$\to $~unselected) preserve sequence length~$k$ but absorb background context from all $N{-}k$ discarded tokens. No new parameters}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{tab:flops}{{4}{6}{FLOPs and KV cache analysis on LLaVA-1.5-7B. Speedup is computed relative to full LLaVA-1.5 inference. End-to-end includes the always-paid CLIP ViT-L/14 encoder cost}{table.4}{}}
\newlabel{sec:results}{{5}{6}{}{section.5}{}}
\citation{chen2024fastv}
\citation{shang2024llava}
\citation{zhang2025sparsevlm}
\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{5}{7}{\textbf {Training curriculum and adaptive-budget schedule.} \emph {Top:} four phases trained sequentially. Phase 0 trains TPN alone with the LLM frozen, forcing meaningful selection before joint co-training. Phase 1 unfreezes LoRA + projector for joint fine-tuning. Phase 1-Adaptive (orange) loads from Phase 1 Final and adds the BudgetPredictor with $\lambda _b$ sweep. Phase 2 (domain transfer to TextVQA) is left as future work. \emph {Bottom:} per-step temperature anneal $\tau {:}1.0{\to }0.3$ (blue) over 40K adaptive steps, with LoRA unfreezing at step 1K (green) and BudgetPredictor activation at step 2K (red)}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{sec:comparison}{{5.3}{7}{}{subsection.5.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:flops}{{6}{7}{Inference FLOPs breakdown. TC-ViTok delivers a $3.39\times $ end-to-end speedup. The added encoder/TPN/merge overhead (blue, $\approx $3.2~GFLOPs) is invisible at this scale —- the LLM prefill dominates by $\geq $700{$\times $}}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{7}{7}{Val-VQA convergence for Phase 1 v3 (4 checkpoints, 50K steps total). Phase 1 Final (single point at 80K, token merge + LoRA r=64) nearly matches the baseline's final val-VQA despite the added merge complexity. Downstream benchmark gains (GQA +4.1\%, POPE +4.1\%) are not reflected here since val-VQA measures only NTP cross-entropy}{figure.7}{}}
\newlabel{tab:main}{{5}{8}{Main results on LLaVA-1.5-7B. Our models use $k{=}144$ tokens (25\% of 576) except Adaptive (variable $k\in [36,288]$). Published VQAv2 numbers from respective papers on val2014. GQA = val\_balanced accuracy (\%). POPE = macro-F1 (\%) across popular/random/adversarial variants. $\dagger $: estimated (Phase 1 v3 was only evaluated on VQAv2 due to compute constraints). \textbf {Bold}: best of ours}{table.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:bars}{{8}{8}{Benchmark comparison across all models. TC-ViTok Final (red) and Adaptive (orange) both \emph {exceed} full LLaVA-1.5 on GQA (61.0\%) despite using only 25\% of tokens — the only metric where our compressed models outperform the uncompressed baseline. v3 GQA and POPE are estimated ($\dagger $)}{figure.8}{}}
\newlabel{tab:pareto}{{6}{8}{Adaptive budget operating points. $k_{\mathrm {eff}}$ is the mean soft token count; hard selection is capped at $k_{\max }{=}288$. Full Pareto sweep ($\lambda _b \in \{0.2, 0.4\}$) is future work}{table.6}{}}
\newlabel{sec:gqa-mechanism}{{5.5}{9}{}{subsection.5.5}{}}
\newlabel{sec:discussion}{{6}{9}{}{section.6}{}}
\newlabel{sec:pooler-failures}{{6.1}{9}{}{subsection.6.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:qualitative}{{9}{9}{\textbf {Conceptual visualization of TPN query-conditioned selection.} Heatmap intensity (warm $=$ high TPN score $\sN $) over the $24{\times }24$ visual grid; crimson borders mark top-$k$ positions selected for the LLM. \textbf {(a)} A spatial-relation query concentrates selection on the two named objects; the remaining $\sim $80\% of the canvas is suppressed. \textbf {(b)} A counting query spreads selection across the shelf row. The same image yields different selections for different queries — the defining property of query conditioning that no query-agnostic baseline (FastV, PruMerge, ToMe) can produce}{figure.9}{}}
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{7}{10}{Ablation study. Val-VQA = NTP cross-entropy (lower is better). Phase 1 v3 $\to $ Final simultaneously changes LoRA rank and adds token merge; isolated ablations per factor are planned. Failure modes from training logs}{table.7}{}}
